#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133495 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ae774f-2496-56c0-a800-450bd8383092" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Inner Product of Two Complex Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "3263f466-0901-58a7-8cd9-ed62cd3036a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5545" :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf y\\in\\mathbb C^3$, compute the standard Hermitian inner product $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle$ of the following two vectors.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\textrm{i} \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x, y⟩ &= x_{1}y_{1}^(―) + x_{2}y_{2}^(―) + x_{3}y_{3}^(―) \\\\\n&= \\textrm{i} \\cdot (-3^(―)) + (-2) \\cdot 4 + \\textrm{i}^(―) + 0 \\cdot 5^(―) \\\\\n&= \\textrm{i} \\cdot (-3) + (-2) \\cdot (4 - \\textrm{i}) + 0 \\\\\n&=-3\\textrm{i} - 8 + 2\\textrm{i} \\\\\n&=-8 - \\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133496 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/id #uuid "b30bd921-7418-5a57-96eb-0c79dd5e4712" :question/math-academy-id "e-5547" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $\\langle \\,\\cdot\\,, \\,\\cdot\\, \\rangle$ over $\\mathbb{C}$.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle\\in\\mathbb R$ for any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$\n2. $\\langle \\mathrm{i}\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, -\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{b} \\rangle$ for any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$\n3. $\\langle\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a},\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$ is a non-negative real number for any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C}$, we have$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\alpha \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\alpha \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle$.\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C}$, we have$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} + \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\alpha\\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\alpha} \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle$.\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$, we have$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle}$.\n- It is positive-definite. For any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n$, we have$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$ is a non-negative real number, and $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = 0$ if and only if $\\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the linearity in the first argument and the antilinearity in the second argument, we have\n$\\langle \\mathrm{i}\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\mathrm{i}\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\overline{\\, \\mathrm{i}\\,} \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a},-\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{b} \\rangle$\nfor any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, by the linearity in the first argument and the antilinearity in the second argument, we have\n$\\langle\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a},\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a} \\rangle =\\mathrm{i}\\cdot \\bar{\\mathrm{i}}\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = -\\mathrm{i}^2\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle= \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$\nfor any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n$. But by positive-definiteness, this is a nonnegative real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133604 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/id #uuid "8d5f83f0-dfb9-5437-a0e5-f33f68a59cec" :question/math-academy-id "e-8578" :question/problem "Given that $\\langle \\,\\cdot\\,, \\,\\cdot\\, \\rangle$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$, find $\\big\\langle -2\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}, \\: \\mathbf{c} \\big\\rangle$ if\n\n$\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = 3+5\\text{i}, \\quad \\quad \\langle \\mathbf{c}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = 2-4\\text{i}$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ satisfies the following properties.\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C}$, we have$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\alpha \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\alpha \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle$.\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C}$, we have$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} + \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\alpha\\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\alpha} \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle$.\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$, we have$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle}$.\n- It is positive-definite. For any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n$, we have$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$ is a non-negative real number, and $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = 0$ if and only if $\\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}$.\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ is linear in the first argument:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨ - 2a + 3b, c⟩ &= ⟨ - 2a, c⟩ + ⟨3b, c⟩ \\\\\n&=-2⟨a, c⟩ + 3⟨b, c⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ has conjugate symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2⟨a, c⟩ + 3⟨b, c⟩ &= -2⟨a, c⟩ + 3⟨c, b⟩^(―)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle$ and $\\langle \\mathbf{c}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2⟨a, c⟩ + 3⟨c, b⟩^(―) &= -2(3 + 5\\textrm{i}) + 3(2 - 4\\textrm{i})^(―) \\\\\n&=-2(3 + 5\\textrm{i}) + 3(2 + 4\\textrm{i}) \\\\\n&=-6 - 10\\textrm{i} + 6 + 12\\textrm{i} \\\\\n&= 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ae774f-2496-56c0-a800-450bd8383092" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Inner Product of Two Complex Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e90e50cc-5a33-547f-8a5e-c9512c042acf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12 - 2\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132537} {:answer/id #uuid "0724d53a-3c99-5942-8ad5-e9b8d8e2227b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 + 5\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 1759218